.Dd November 24, 2026
.Dt SYSCLEAN 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sysclean
.Nd list and purge obsolete elements between OpenBSD upgrades
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a | p
.Op Fl i
.Nm
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl i
.Brq Fl n | x
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a
.Xr perl 1
script designed to help remove obsolete files, users and groups, between
.Ox
upgrades.
.Pp
.Nm
compares a reference installation against the currently installed elements,
taking files from both the base system and packages into account.
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
is a read-only tool.
It does not remove anything on the system.
This can be changed with the
.Fl x
option, which applies a cleanup plan to the running system.
The
.Fl n
option shows the plan using the same inventory and selection rules.
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
lists obsolete filenames, users and groups on the system.
It excludes any used dynamic libraries.
It will report base libraries with versions newer than what's expected.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
All files mode.
.Nm
will not exclude filenames used by installed packages from output.
.It Fl i
With ignored.
In the default read-only mode,
.Nm
will include filenames that are ignored by default, using
.Pa /etc/sysclean.ignore .
In a cleanup plan
.Pq Fl n No or Fl x ,
the meaning is inverted:
elements listed in
.Pa /etc/changelist
and
.Pa /etc/sysclean.ignore
are respected and are not removed.
Hard-coded ignores remain active in all modes.
.It Fl n
Preview the cleanup plan.
Use the same
.Fl a
and
.Fl i
flags in the preview and apply invocations.
Path preflight runs during preview.
Account actions remain subject to checks against the live system at execution.
This option is incompatible with
.Fl p
and
.Fl x .
.It Fl p
Package mode.
.Nm
will output package names that are using obsolete files.
.It Fl x
Apply mode.
.Nm
will remove the obsolete elements it reports instead of only listing them.
Files are unlinked and obsolete directories are removed with
.Xr rmdir 2
only when they are empty.
Obsolete users and groups are removed with
.Xr userdel 8
and
.Xr groupdel 8 .
User home directories are preserved.
A group still used as a primary group is refused.
Local
.Xr userdel 8
preservation settings can retain a disabled account.
Supported account fields are updated with
.Xr usermod 8
when the matching reference authorizes the change.
For an existing package account, the PLIST must mark a field as required
before it can authorize an update.
An account whose name matches the reference installation but whose
uid or gid differs is reported but is not removed or modified.
.Pp
The
.Fl x
option is not compatible with the
.Fl p
option.
It may be combined with
.Fl a ;
libraries used by installed packages are listed in read-only mode and are
protected from removal in apply mode when they are recorded in the package
metadata.
When
.Fl i
is used, elements ignored through
.Pa /etc/changelist
and
.Pa /etc/sysclean.ignore
are respected and never removed;
without
.Fl i ,
they are removed like any other obsolete element.
The hard-coded ignore list in the program is always respected,
regardless of
.Fl i .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "PKG_DBDIR"
.It Ev PKG_DBDIR
The standard package database directory,
.Pa /var/db/pkg ,
can be overridden by specifying an alternative directory in the
.Ev PKG_DBDIR
environment variable for ordinary queries.
Plans made with
.Fl n
or
.Fl x
use the host package database.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/sysclean.ignore"
.It Pa /etc/sysclean.ignore
Files to ignore, one per line, with absolute pathnames.
.Pp
A line starting with
.Sq @user
or
.Sq @group
will be interpreted as user or group to ignore.
.Pp
A line of the form
.Sq @include
followed by a quoted file name loads another ignore file.
Relative include paths are resolved from
.Pa / .
A missing included file is an error.
.Pp
Shell globbing is supported in pathnames; see
.Xr File::Glob 3p .
If the pattern matches a directory,
.Nm
will not inspect it or any files contained within.
For compatibility with the
.Xr changelist 5
file format, the character
.Sq +
is skipped at the beginning of a line.
.Pp
.Pa /etc/changelist
is implicitly included.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Obtain a list of outdated files (without libraries used by packages):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysclean
/usr/lib/libc.so.83.0
.Ed
.Pp
Obtain a list of old libraries and the package using them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysclean -p
/usr/lib/libc.so.84.1   git-2.7.0
/usr/lib/libc.so.84.1   gmake-4.1p0
.Ed
.Pp
Obtain a list of all outdated files (including used libraries):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysclean -a
/usr/lib/libc.so.83.0
/usr/lib/libc.so.84.1
.Ed
.Pp
List account candidates for review:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysclean
@user _mpd:560:_mpd::/var/spool/mpd:/sbin/nologin
@group _mpd:560
.Ed
.Pp
Preview the cleanup while honoring local exclusions, then apply after
reviewing the plan:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysclean -ni
[INFO] planned removal: /usr/lib/libc.so.83.0
# sysclean -xi
[INFO] unlink /usr/lib/libc.so.83.0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pkg_info 1 ,
.Xr sysmerge 8
.Sh HISTORY
The first version of
.Nm
was written as
.Xr ksh 1
script in 2015, and rewritten using
.Xr perl 1
in 2016.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An -nosplit
.Nm
was written by
.An Sebastien Marie Aq Mt semarie@kapouay.eu.org .
.Sh CAVEATS
The
.Fl x
option modifies the running system.
Review a fresh
.Fl n
plan before applying.
The reference data may omit legitimate local files and accounts.
A candidate immediately under
.Pa /
causes the plan to abort before mutation, since the scan gives that
directory a read-only unveil rule.
.Pp
.Nm
removes directories only when empty.
The final component of a symbolic link is unlinked without following it.
An ancestor symbolic link or a detected change in object identity
causes a candidate to be refused.
The final validation and removal are separate operations.
.Pp
In apply mode,
.Nm
exits with status 1 if an action fails or is refused.
Successful changes from earlier actions remain in place.
